# Credentials and tokens -- never commit these.
.env
*.token.json
.movein_triage_token.json

# THE REAL CONFIGURATION.
#
# config_local.py holds colleagues' email addresses and the property name. The
# public config.py carries example.com placeholders and imports this file at the
# end if it exists, so the repo stays publishable while the installed copy works
# against the real mailbox.
config_local.py

# THE RESIDENT DATABASE AND ITS KEY.
#
# The live database lives outside this directory entirely
# (~/Library/Application Support/MoveInTriage/), specifically so that no command
# run inside the repo can sweep it into a commit. These patterns are a second
# line of defence in case a database is ever created here by a test, a stray
# MOVEIN_DB_PATH, or a copy made while debugging.
#
# The contents are encrypted, but that is not a reason to relax: an encrypted
# blob in a public repository is a standing invitation to whoever eventually
# gets the key, and the unit numbers and timestamps in it are plaintext.
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite3-journal
*.sqlite3-wal
*.sqlite3-shm
*.db
db.key
*.key

# Generated output. The board is a render of live data; it does not belong in
# version control, and committing it would put unit-level status into git history.
board.html
board_preview.png

# py2app build output
build/
dist/

# Debugging scaffolding from working out why the status item would not render on
# macOS 26. Kept on disk because they are genuinely useful if it breaks again,
# but not worth committing.
Diagnose*.command
Check.command
Run From Source.command
*.log

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/
